WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.152
The passport crisis.

00:00:02.102 --> 00:00:07.636
In 2022 and 2023, Canadians waited months for passport renewals.

00:00:07.636 --> 00:00:10.647
Lineups stretched around government buildings.

00:00:10.647 --> 00:00:12.454
Flights were missed.

00:00:12.454 --> 00:00:15.363
Family emergencies went unresolved.

00:00:15.363 --> 00:00:22.784
The same government that processes over four hundred thousand immigration applications per year could not renew passports for its own citizens.

00:00:22.784 --> 00:00:29.249
The Auditor General documented the staffing failures, the outdated technology, and the institutional dysfunction.

00:00:29.249 --> 00:00:30.874
What happened.

00:00:30.874 --> 00:00:38.522
During 2022 and 2023, passport processing times ballooned from the standard twenty business days to months-long waits.

00:00:38.522 --> 00:00:42.329
Service Canada offices saw lineups forming before dawn.

00:00:42.329 --> 00:00:46.147
Citizens camped overnight outside passport offices.

00:00:46.147 --> 00:00:53.238
The crisis was not caused by unprecedented demand — passport renewal volumes were predictable based on ten-year expiry cycles.

00:00:53.238 --> 00:00:58.545
It was caused by a failure to plan for the post-pandemic surge despite months of advance warning.

00:00:58.545 --> 00:01:00.011
The pattern.

00:01:00.011 --> 00:01:03.978
Phoenix Pay: a system launched despite internal warnings.

00:01:03.978 --> 00:01:07.977
ArriveCAN: a procurement outsourced to a two-person firm.

00:01:07.977 --> 00:01:12.159
Passport crisis: predictable demand met by zero preparation.

00:01:12.159 --> 00:01:18.147
Each time, the AG documents the failure, the government promises reform, and no one is disciplined.

00:01:18.147 --> 00:01:21.284
The pattern is not about individual failures.

00:01:21.284 --> 00:01:29.874
It is about an institutional architecture that is structurally incapable of delivering basic services to citizens while simultaneously expanding new programs.
